collaborator_evidence local git history only -- commits 26197f6ad6, 35b624e798, 2c905b5318 landed by peer hands before this lap opened; ListAgents named no reachable agent and the process table showed no concurrent run
collaborator_scope the glow and yonder link repoints, the equinox family's room, template-manifest.bron -- all left as HEAD carries them
title the box that filled twice -- %328, and a whole lap recovered out of it
prompt Open as HUSH under the INNER LOOP Standing block -- roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, QA on touch, council rota row N mod 5, prose witnesses on prose sends, stamp-and-name, ASCII-first, READ SCOPE -- pick up the running thread and work Pond completing the ai-jail enclosure under the accrete-only gate boundary.
think ListAgents named no peer, so the charter law's first act came back empty; git log named three peer commits landed since my last lap and I attributed by history rather than by process. Then git stash list, which the Standing block asks for since %321, and the box held TWO entries. stash@{1} was the hush-crash leavings %321 already recovered -- every file identical to HEAD or older, so nothing to take. stash@{0} was stamped 20260828-171428, forty-four seconds before my own open, and held a whole finished lap: a scan, a control, a witness, a launcher heal, a ledger row, and its own session log.
think That is %321 firing a second time, three hours after it closed. Its repair was a written habit -- a lap opens with git stash list -- and the habit worked exactly as designed: I ran it and found the mail. What it cannot do is work for the lap that forgets, and the runner's own header already names the ladder this tree climbs -- rule, then reading, then refusal. %321 seated the rule. The second firing is what a lantern firing twice looks like.
think Reds first, so the cold roster opened before anything moved: 79 guards, 74 green, 5 red, tree_moved=no over twenty-eight minutes. I held still the whole time, which is why the digest matched at both ends and the reading describes one tree. foundations_link was mine to close -- the peer's last commit moved the equinox family into tools/equinox/almanac/ and one living foundation kept citing tools/gen/chapter/. The whole-tree sweep found 311 citers of the departed room and every one but that file is dated testimony, resolved rather than rewritten. Foundations are the named exception: their basenames carry stamps and the guard that gates them at zero exists because reading a stamped foundation as testimony broke 38 links once (%124).
think Recovery used git checkout <stash> -- <path> rather than git show <stash>:<path>, which is %321's own closing lesson and the exec-bit law: the tree object carries mode, the blob does not. Both shell scans arrived 100755 and the rish witness 100644, which is right -- a witness is run with its interpreter named ahead of it. I took only what HEAD had not moved: the three new state-guard files, the launcher heal, the example, the pin scan, pond/README.md, enclosure-editors.md and the ledger. glow/README.md, the active-designing pages, template-manifest.bron and the merit ledger I left as HEAD carries them, because the peer had landed better answers there -- SKILL.md reclassified personal after measuring the seed-link guard rise, where the stash still carried allow.
think The two peer-moved pins I applied by hand rather than overwriting: the roster row appended at the end where HEAD's tail sits, and the index row inserted under 170657 in newest-first order rather than at the top. pond_enclosure_state GREEN on metal, 24 control cases, 2.5 seconds, and its scan reads this pier honestly -- pinned_elders=0 gated, host_pinned_elders=2 and stranded_rooms=2 reported.
think The loom for the box went on line one of the runner, beside staged_uncommitted, because that is the one place every lap already looks and the lap that needs it most is the lap that did not remember to look. It reports and never gates: fleet_round_open.sh parks a dirty tree there by design, and a guard that reds on ordinary work is a guard someone turns off. Each entry carries its message and file count, since a bare number is what %321 already had and nobody opened.
think Writing the control cases found something bigger than the cases. Twenty-one of the control's forty-six behaviors were already reading no at HEAD, every one of them runner-driven. The root: standing_equipment_run.sh appends to construction/standing-equipment-hitrate.kyri and writes construction/standing-equipment-receipt.kyri, and a bare >> cannot create a parent directory, so in a pen the append died and took the runner with it before a guard ran. Both writes entered at e1c50fcbae, 20260826.222031, and the runner wrote nothing under construction/ before that commit.
think Why nobody saw it for two days: standing_equipment_witness.rish is on no roster and has no caller in the tree -- grep finds its own header, its two scripts naming it as their driver, and dated logs. Its last recorded green is 20260826.134505 at 46 behaviors, eight hours before the meter landed. And it is unrostered for a structural reason rather than an oversight: the scan refuses under roster_broken whenever the run card records any red, so a rostered self-witness would write its own red onto the card its scan reads and that red would sustain itself. I booked the seat rather than taking it.
think The fix refused the obvious shape. Creating the directory would work and would be wrong: an untracked construction/ written into a pen moves the very tree digest three of those cases exist to read. So the write is skipped where its room is absent and the skip says so on its own line, because a silent skip is how the reading goes quietly false the day construction/ moves.
obs The control paid for itself inside one lap. one_letter_sized stayed red after the pen fix, and the reason was mine: git stash show --name-only omits untracked files while fleet_round_open.sh stashes with -u, so a lap whose leavings are all NEW files -- a fresh scan, a fresh witness, fresh fixtures, which is exactly what %321 lost -- would have read 0 files and looked like an empty envelope. Proven in a pen on git 2.54.0 at 0 without --include-untracked and 2 with it. A case written to agree with the reading would have passed; a case written to prove it bit.
obs The second failure of that same case was the pen's own run card being swept into the stash, three files where the case plants two. Removing it before the push made the count the case's rather than a leftover of the reading before it -- and that is the same class as the finding above, one layer smaller: state left by an earlier reading changing what a later one measures.
obs The ledger had two bytes of headroom, so %328 could not be written until the pin folded. Only %321 and %322 read CLOSED; every other row in the pin is OPEN and stays by law. They fold together honestly -- both are about a record that was true and unread -- and the shelf says so. Pin 24,574 to 19,662, then 23,881 with the new row, 695 under bound. Spine GREEN at 324 with no gaps once the headline's three numbers were moved with it.
obs The rota read row 2 by the computed reading -- N is git rev-list --count HEAD at 3637, and 3637 mod 5 is 2 -- with no repeat to advance past, since the prior two laps read rows 4 and 3. Fire, Mars, cut and stop, and the sense is vision. The row named the lap before I chose it: Standfast is the hand on the cord, reds-first is where the hours go once it is pulled, and the three depths of removal is the fold I took at the shallowest depth that served. Its own line -- the lap that sees clearly cuts once, the lap that squints cuts twice -- is why I ran the control from git show HEAD: before believing the 21 were mine.
obs Said plainly rather than claimed green: standing_equipment_witness.rish still reds, and not on my work. Its scan refuses while the run card holds declared_ceiling and empty_document, both Mind's, so the witness cannot reach its own assertions on this pier today. My sixteen new cases are proven by running the control directly -- control_verdict=ok, zero =no, on metal -- and that is the honest claim rather than a witness green I did not earn. readme_metrics regenerates in the pre-commit hook and remember_git_nib closes with this commit's nib.
obs The send met the derived-spine law head on. xy had moved six commits while I worked, and the rebase brought five conflicts. Upstream had booked its OWN %323, %324 and %325 at 20260828.175530 -- different reds, different stamps, mine unpushed and theirs published. So my two rows renumbered to %327 and %328 while keeping their stamps, which is exactly what the law says a number is: a view the anointed remote allocates, where the stamp is the identity. Nine files carried the elder numbers in prose and all nine were swept; the only %323 and %324 left in the tree are in a peer's log, where they correctly mean upstream's rows.
obs Upstream had also folded %321 and %322 onto its own shelf while I folded them onto mine, so my shelf and recital row were redundant and I dropped both rather than ship a second fold of the same rows. My foundations_link repair collided with a peer's identical one -- git merged it silently because we wrote the same target -- so that repair is theirs and mine equally and adds nothing to this diff.
obs The pin could not hold what the law says it must. Upstream's REDS.md arrived at 21,281 bytes with SIX rows and every one of them OPEN, leaving 3,295 for two rows that wanted 6,429. Nothing could fold, because the pin keeps what is open; nothing could ship over, because pin_bound_touch refuses the commit. So both rows were rewritten short -- three fields, the measurements, the repair, the proof, and nothing else -- across five passes, and they fit at 24,574 of 24,576. That is the seat the card already named as urgent arriving as a wall rather than a warning: the open set alone now exceeds the bound, so the next red genuinely has nowhere to go.
obs Nothing was dropped. stash@{1} is superseded in every file and I left it in the box rather than dropping it, because dropping a stash is a small irreversible act this lap did not need to take. stash@{0} keeps everything I did not lift, so the peer-landed halves I declined stay one git checkout away.
